 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Linear with respect to the number of leaf voxels $N_{leaves}$. Each leaf is processed in constant time by the geometry shader.
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Low. Only leaf voxel data needs to be stored in vertex buffers. The octree structure itself is stored in memory but not directly rendered.
-*   **Performance:** Efficient for sparse datasets where the number of visible leaves is small compared to the total volume. Performance degrades if the scene is dense and nearly all leaves are visible, as the geometry shader overhead becomes significant.
-*   **Strengths:** Simple implementation using standard OpenGL geometry shaders. Effective culling reduces overdraw.
-*   **Limitations:** Geometry shader overhead can be high on some hardware. Not suitable for very dense volumes without additional optimization (like the child method). Transparency handling requires sorting or alpha blending, which is not detailed in the source.
+*   **Time Complexity:** Linear with respect to the number of leaf voxels, as each leaf generates one geometry shader invocation [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Low, as it only stores leaf voxel data and does not require ray-marching structures [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Performance:** Fast on modern GPUs due to hardware rasterization and geometry shaders [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Efficient visualization of large sparse voxel datasets; simple implementation using standard OpenGL [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Square splats may not accurately represent spherical or complex voxel shapes; no explicit depth sorting is mentioned in the basic pipeline, potentially causing z-fighting or incorrect blending without additional techniques [S1, unofficial].
+*   **When to use:** Real-time visualization of sparse voxel data where approximate volume representation is sufficient [S1, unofficial].
+*   **When not to use:** When high-fidelity ray-traced rendering or accurate occlusion is required [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-The method was implemented as part of a sparse voxel octree renderer using OpenGL and C++ [S1, unofficial]. It relies on SVOs generated by the `ooc_svo_builder` tool by Forceflow [S1, unofficial]. The approach leverages the programmable graphics pipeline to handle the conversion from volumetric data to geometric primitives on the GPU.
+The method was developed as part of the `svo_renderer` project, which aims to render sparse voxel octrees generated by `ooc_svo_builder` [S1, unofficial]. It leverages the programmable graphics pipeline (OpenGL) to offload the rendering workload to the GPU [S1, unofficial].
 
